<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The backend is a FastAPI server on a laptop on the same LAN, over plain
    HTTP. Android 9 (API 28) and later block cleartext by default, which is why
    this is needed on a phone and never was on Pepper: the robot's tablet is
    API 23, where cleartext is permitted and this file is ignored entirely.
    Adding it therefore changes nothing about the robot build.

    Cleartext is permitted broadly rather than scoped to private addresses,
    because Android matches <domain> entries by hostname suffix and has no
    notion of IP ranges or CIDR. Listing "192.168.0.0" would match only that
    one literal address, not the 192.168.x.x network - a scoped config would
    break every LAN address except the one written down, and the laptop's
    address changes.

    The trade-off is real: the app will also send cleartext to a public host if
    pointed at one. That is acceptable for a research tool on a trusted
    network, and it is why the backend must not be exposed publicly. If the API
    is ever served over HTTPS, delete this file and the manifest attributes.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
